<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alexander Jannaeus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 76 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> · Also known as Alexander Yannai · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasmonean-dynasty">Hasmonean dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Alexander Jannaeus was king and high priest of the Hasmonean kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaea">Judaea</a> from 103 to 76 BCE. Through constant wars he extended the kingdom to its greatest size, taking coastal cities and lands east of the Jordan. His harsh suppression of a Pharisee-led revolt made his reign one of internal strife.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alexander-jannaeus.jpg" alt="Alexander Jannaeus" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Georges Reverdy · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alexander_Jannaeus_medal.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Alexander Jannaeus, born Jonathan, was the third son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-hyrcanus">John Hyrcanus</a> by his second wife. His half-brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristobulus-i">Aristobulus I</a> imprisoned him for the sake of his own security, and when Aristobulus died after a reign of one year, his widow released Alexander and his brothers in 103 BCE. His wife was Salome Alexandra, who came from a Pharisaic family. Their sons were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyrcanus-ii">Hyrcanus II</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristobulus-ii">Aristobulus II</a>.</p><p>Like his father, Alexander served as high priest. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharisees">Pharisees</a> objected to the union of the two offices, and said he was unfit to serve because his mother had been captured and raped at Modi'in. The king was enraged and persecuted them for the rest of his life. The main sources for his reign are the Antiquities of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> and The Jewish War by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Josephus</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Alexander's early campaigns drew him into a war with Ptolemy IX Lathyros, who had a base on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>. Ptolemy defeated him at Asophon, near the Jordan, and overran much of the kingdom, but Ptolemy's mother Cleopatra III intervened and the Judean king kept his throne. After her death he campaigned again, capturing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gadara">Gadara</a>, Raphia and Anthedon, and defeating Gaza around 96 BCE; he is said to have massacred those who had taken refuge in the Temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a> there.</p><p>Against the Nabataean king Obodas I he suffered a severe defeat in the Golan, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camel</a>-mounted troops ambushed him. At home, during the Feast of Tabernacles, he poured the water libation on his feet instead of the altar, and the crowd pelted him with citrons. He killed six thousand people. The civil war that followed drew in Demetrius III Eucaerus, who beat Alexander at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shechem">Shechem</a>, but Alexander won in the end and had eight hundred rebels, mainly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharisees">Pharisees</a>, crucified in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In his last campaigns, from 83 to 80 BCE, Alexander took cities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaulanitis">Gaulanitis</a> and Galaaditis, including Golan, Gamala, Pella, Dium and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerash">Gerasa</a>. At its largest the kingdom covered most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> coast of the Land of Israel and the lands around the Jordan. He died around 76 BCE during the siege of Ragaba, after three years of illness brought on by heavy drinking and quartan ague. He named his wife Salome Alexandra as his successor.</p><p>Menahem Stern held that the weakness of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a> and Roman restraint made the expansion possible. Aryeh Kasher argues that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Josephus</a> relied on hostile sources and that the wars grew from long-standing hostility between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> and the Hellenistic cities. Jannaeus's coins are the most abundant Jewish coinage of the period; Kenneth Atkinson reads their low quality as a sign of economic instability.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Alexander Jannaeus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>John Hyrcanus conquered Idumaea and destroyed the Samaritan temple on Mount Gerizim; <b>Alexander Jannaeus</b> extended the realm across the Jordan.</blockquote><a href="/state/hasmonean-kingdom">Hasmonean Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
